  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Upper level ridging over the eastern \nPacific will continue to keep dry and very warm temperatures over \nNevada this afternoon. By this evening, the upper ridge will begin\nto move east out of Nevada. Winds will be from the northwest with\ngusts up to 25 to 30 mph. High temperatures expected to be above \nnormal in the 90s with overnight lows in the 40s to 50s. \n\nThursday, models are showing the upper ridge moving somewhere into\nthe four-corners region. This will shift from a northwesterly flow\nto a more southerly flow across central Nevada and will bring in\nsome mid-level moisture. Precipitable water values are steadily\nincreasing with each model run, reaching up to 0.5 to 0.6 inches\nwith weak instability in the upper levels. CAPE values staying\nfairly consistent between 100-200 J/kg. Will keep isolated dry\nthunderstorms in the forecast for central Nevada. Winds expected \nto be light below 15 mph, however wind gusts up to 50 mph are \npossible with the development of thunderstorms in the area. Highs \nwill still be above normal in the 90s and lows in the 50s.\n\nFriday, the upper ridge continues to slowly move eastward keeping\nthe southerly flow over Nevada while a weak upper trough forms\nover the eastern Pacific. The southerly flow will continue to push\nin more moisture northward across central and northern Nevada.\nChances for mix of wet/dry thunderstorms will increase by the \nafternoon starting from the northwest and moving east. Much of \ncentral Nevada from N. Nye, White Pine and southern Eureka \ncounties are expected to stay dry as southerly flow is expected to\nincrease winds with gusts up to 35-40 mph which will prevent \nmoisture from reaching overhead. Fire Weather Watch has been \nissued across the region due to dry thunderstorms in the north, \nand wind/low RH in central regions.\n\nSaturday, the upper trough will weaken, shifting the upper flow to\na more west direction. Chances for showers and thunderstorms will\nremain in northern Nevada north of the I-80 corridor. Confidence\nat this time is low, there is still chance for some isolated dry\nthunderstorms. Temperatures expected to be slightly cooler \ncompared to recent days with highs in the 80s. Winds will be\nbreezy with west/northwesterly winds at 10-15 mph, gusts 25 mph.\nOvernight lows in the 40s.\n\nSunday through Wednesday, models are showing upper ridging\nbuilding back up over the southwest, warming high temperatures\nback into the 90s with lows in the 50s.",
